WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:03.560
Right now, NASA is exploring the sun

00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:05.290
like never before.

00:00:05.290 --> 00:00:09.300
In 2018, it launched
the Parker Solar Probe,

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:12.940
which is swooping to within
6.2 million kilometers

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:14.514
of the Sun's surface,

00:00:14.514 --> 00:00:16.881
the closest we've ever been.

00:00:16.881 --> 00:00:19.923
But what if we wanted an even closer look?

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:26.920
Our first stop gets pretty hot.

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:29.420
At 7 million to 10 million kilometers

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:32.770
above the sun's surface,
we reach the corona,

00:00:32.770 --> 00:00:35.080
the outermost layer of the sun.

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:38.630
It blazes at 1 million degrees Celsius,

00:00:38.630 --> 00:00:41.750
nearly 900 times as hot as lava.

00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:44.675
And it's tens of thousands
of times brighter

00:00:44.675 --> 00:00:46.410
here than on Earth.

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:48.468
Now, the probe's heat shield

00:00:48.468 --> 00:00:50.801
works like a very good mirror,

00:00:50.801 --> 00:00:55.210
reflecting 99.9% of the incoming light.

00:00:55.210 --> 00:00:59.210
But we'll need something
even better as we get closer.

00:00:59.210 --> 00:01:02.330
At about 3,000 kilometers
above the surface,

00:01:02.330 --> 00:01:04.380
we reach the chromosphere,

00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:06.540
the second layer of the sun.

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:08.631
See that massive plume?

00:01:08.631 --> 00:01:11.281
That's called a solar prominence.

00:01:11.281 --> 00:01:13.600
These loops of gas are suspended

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:15.847
by a powerful magnetic field

00:01:15.847 --> 00:01:18.380
and stretched for tens of
thousands of kilometers

00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:19.630
beyond the sun.

00:01:19.630 --> 00:01:23.670
And they can reach over
10,000 degrees Celsius,

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:26.830
exactly the sort of
obstacle you'd want to avoid

00:01:26.830 --> 00:01:29.860
when flying a spacecraft into the sun.

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:32.960
And the next layer is just as perilous:

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:34.560
the photosphere.

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:38.290
This is the surface of
the sun we see every day.

00:01:38.290 --> 00:01:41.450
Down here, you'll start
to feel pretty lousy,

00:01:41.450 --> 00:01:44.580
because the sun's gravity is so strong,

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:47.290
a 150-pound person on Earth

00:01:47.290 --> 00:01:50.200
would weigh about 4,000 pounds here.

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:52.760
That's nearly the same as a rhino.

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:54.276
If you could land here,

00:01:54.276 --> 00:01:57.034
all that extra weight
would crush your bones

00:01:57.034 --> 00:01:59.670
and pulverize your internal organs.

00:01:59.670 --> 00:02:01.800
But if you take a look around,

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:04.850
there's nothing here for
you to actually land on,

00:02:04.850 --> 00:02:08.950
because the sun doesn't have
any solid surface to speak of.

00:02:08.950 --> 00:02:13.270
It's just a giant ball of
hydrogen and helium gas.

00:02:13.270 --> 00:02:15.890
So instead of landing on the photosphere,

00:02:15.890 --> 00:02:18.230
you're going to sink into it.

00:02:18.230 --> 00:02:20.600
One of the biggest
dangers in the photosphere

00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:23.330
comes from these enormous black spots

00:02:23.330 --> 00:02:25.500
you can see as you look around.

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:27.670
These are called sunspots.

00:02:27.670 --> 00:02:29.650
They're cooler regions of gas,

00:02:29.650 --> 00:02:32.710
some as large as the entire Earth.

00:02:32.710 --> 00:02:36.300
The sunspots are produced
by powerful magnetic fields

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:38.240
coming from inside the sun,

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:42.040
which, on one hand, would
fry your electronics,

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:45.267
but more importantly,
where a sunspot forms,

00:02:45.267 --> 00:02:48.103
a solar flare often follows.

00:02:48.103 --> 00:02:50.980
That's when magnetic
fields and superhot gas

00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:53.360
violently erupt from the surface,

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:55.551
releasing as much energy as

00:02:55.551 --> 00:02:58.376
10 billion hydrogen bombs.

00:02:58.376 --> 00:03:01.640
So let's steer clear
of those active regions

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:04.800
and make our way to the sun's interior.

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:08.203
Just beneath the surface
is the convective zone.

00:03:08.203 --> 00:03:09.945
Here, temperatures reach

00:03:09.945 --> 00:03:12.661
2 million degrees Celsius.

00:03:12.661 --> 00:03:15.950
That's hotter than your heat
shield was designed to handle.

00:03:15.950 --> 00:03:18.640
In fact, there's no material on Earth

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:20.980
that could withstand this heat.

00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:24.770
The best we've got is a compound
called tantalum carbide,

00:03:24.770 --> 00:03:29.290
which can handle about
4,000 degrees Celsius max.

00:03:29.290 --> 00:03:32.620
On Earth, we use it to
coat jet-engine blades.

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:35.630
So even if we made it this far,

00:03:35.630 --> 00:03:38.750
we couldn't actually survive down here.

00:03:38.750 --> 00:03:42.120
But for curiosity's
sake, let's keep going.

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:44.878
At 200,000 kilometers down,

00:03:44.878 --> 00:03:47.153
we hit the radiative zone.

00:03:47.153 --> 00:03:49.950
This is the thickest layer of the sun.

00:03:49.950 --> 00:03:53.790
It makes up almost half
of the entire radius,

00:03:53.790 --> 00:03:56.128
so we'll be spending some time here,

00:03:56.128 --> 00:03:59.270
which isn't great, because the pressure

00:03:59.270 --> 00:04:02.060
is at least 100 million times greater

00:04:02.060 --> 00:04:04.190
than at sea level on Earth.

00:04:04.190 --> 00:04:07.400
Because it's so dense,
there's not much room

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:09.270
for light waves to travel,

00:04:09.270 --> 00:04:10.870
which means down here,

00:04:10.870 --> 00:04:12.945
it's pitch black.

00:04:12.945 --> 00:04:15.540
Instead of traveling
across the radiative zone

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:17.056
and hitting your eye,

00:04:17.056 --> 00:04:19.410
the light waves slam into electrons

00:04:19.410 --> 00:04:21.760
and other particles in the plasma.

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:24.340
And some even rebound inward

00:04:24.340 --> 00:04:27.720
towards our last stop, the core.

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:30.770
500,000 kilometers below the surface,

00:04:30.770 --> 00:04:32.523
the center of the sun makes up

00:04:32.523 --> 00:04:35.280
nearly a quarter of its radius.

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:37.450
Down here, the pressure has risen

00:04:37.450 --> 00:04:40.450
to more than 200 billion
times the pressure

00:04:40.450 --> 00:04:42.090
at sea level on Earth,

00:04:42.090 --> 00:04:45.420
pressing the surrounding
atoms so closely together

00:04:45.420 --> 00:04:48.930
that it's about 10 times denser than iron.

00:04:48.930 --> 00:04:52.860
Plus, it's a blistering 15
million degrees Celsius,

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:57.350
making it the hottest place
in the entire solar system.

00:04:57.350 --> 00:04:58.750
Which makes sense,

00:04:58.750 --> 00:05:01.910
because almost all of
the sun's immense energy

00:05:01.910 --> 00:05:03.840
is produced in the core.

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:06.060
That's right, we're traveling through

00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:09.280
the powerhouse of the sun itself.

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:11.790
Now, contrary to popular belief,

00:05:11.790 --> 00:05:14.690
the sun is not actually on fire.

00:05:14.690 --> 00:05:17.340
Instead, all that energy is created

00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:19.170
through a nuclear reaction,

00:05:19.170 --> 00:05:21.690
which slams hydrogen atoms together

00:05:21.690 --> 00:05:24.110
to create larger helium atoms

00:05:24.110 --> 00:05:26.710
and some extra energy on the side.

00:05:26.710 --> 00:05:30.230
So even if you managed to
survive the blistering heat,

00:05:30.230 --> 00:05:33.340
the solar flares, and
the crushing pressure,

00:05:33.340 --> 00:05:34.710
you'd now have to climb

00:05:34.710 --> 00:05:38.350
out of the solar system's
biggest nuclear reactor.

00:05:38.350 --> 00:05:41.480
Let's just say the odds
are not in your favor.

00:05:41.480 --> 00:05:44.020
Maybe our closest encounter to the sun

00:05:44.020 --> 00:05:45.937
should be on the beach.

